1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:02,120
You order a jacket online.

2
00:00:02,120 --> 00:00:03,970
It arrives on a Tuesday.

3
00:00:04,350 --> 00:00:07,540
By Thursday, you've decided
the sleeves are too short.

4
00:00:07,920 --> 00:00:09,060
So you send it back.

5
00:00:09,060 --> 00:00:10,540
The label is free.

6
00:00:10,540 --> 00:00:13,050
The refund shows up a few days later.

7
00:00:13,430 --> 00:00:15,330
For you, the story ends there.

8
00:00:15,330 --> 00:00:17,740
For the jacket, it's just getting started.

9
00:00:18,120 --> 00:00:22,030
It will be scanned, trucked,
sorted, inspected, and judged.

10
00:00:22,030 --> 00:00:25,310
And every one of those steps
costs somebody something.

11
00:00:25,690 --> 00:00:28,010
So who actually pays for a free return?

12
00:00:28,910 --> 00:00:30,120
Start with the scale.

13
00:00:30,500 --> 00:00:35,710
In October 2025, the National Retail
Federation and Happy Returns,

14
00:00:35,710 --> 00:00:41,870
a returns company owned by UPS,
published a report based on two surveys:

15
00:00:41,870 --> 00:00:45,903
one of shoppers, one of
large American retailers.

16
00:00:46,283 --> 00:00:49,883
Those retailers estimated that about 15.8

17
00:00:49,883 --> 00:00:53,583
percent of their sales
would come back in 2025.

18
00:00:53,963 --> 00:00:55,903
Across the whole industry,

19
00:00:55,903 --> 00:01:02,583
the report projected $849.9 billion
worth of returned merchandise.

20
00:01:02,963 --> 00:01:08,553
Online, the estimate was higher:
about 19.3 percent of online sales.

21
00:01:08,933 --> 00:01:11,163
Keep in mind what those numbers are.

22
00:01:11,163 --> 00:01:16,043
They measure the value of goods sent
back, not the cost of processing them.

23
00:01:16,043 --> 00:01:20,273
And they're projections for the
United States, not a final count.

24
00:01:20,653 --> 00:01:22,633
Shoppers are paying attention.

25
00:01:22,633 --> 00:01:24,113
In the same report,

26
00:01:24,113 --> 00:01:28,033
82 percent of consumers
said free returns are an

27
00:01:28,033 --> 00:01:30,803
important consideration
when shopping online.

28
00:01:31,183 --> 00:01:34,723
And 57 percent said they had
decided not to shop with a

29
00:01:34,723 --> 00:01:38,184
retailer after it started
charging for returns.

30
00:01:38,564 --> 00:01:41,434
So retailers are caught
between two pressures.

31
00:01:41,434 --> 00:01:46,244
Returns are expensive, and making
them harder can push customers away.

32
00:01:47,144 --> 00:01:48,604
Let's follow the jacket.

33
00:01:48,604 --> 00:01:52,384
What comes next is one common
path, not the only one.

34
00:01:52,764 --> 00:01:53,684
Step one: the hand-off.

35
00:01:53,684 --> 00:01:58,254
Depending on the store, you might
mail it, leave it with a carrier,

36
00:01:58,254 --> 00:02:02,404
or bring it to a counter,
sometimes without a box or a label.

37
00:02:02,784 --> 00:02:07,104
In NRF's survey, more
retailers offered box-free,

38
00:02:07,104 --> 00:02:10,884
label-free drop-offs in
2025 than a year earlier.

39
00:02:11,264 --> 00:02:13,534
Step two: consolidation.

40
00:02:13,534 --> 00:02:16,344
Instead of shipping every
item back on its own,

41
00:02:16,344 --> 00:02:20,834
some return networks pool many returns
together and ship them in bulk.

42
00:02:21,214 --> 00:02:24,224
Step three: a warehouse or returns center,

43
00:02:24,224 --> 00:02:27,574
where someone opens the package
and scans what's inside.

44
00:02:27,954 --> 00:02:29,054
Step four: inspection.

45
00:02:29,054 --> 00:02:30,614
Is it the right item?

46
00:02:30,614 --> 00:02:31,664
Is it damaged?

47
00:02:31,664 --> 00:02:32,714
Has it been worn?

48
00:02:32,714 --> 00:02:34,404
Are the tags still on?

49
00:02:34,784 --> 00:02:36,274
Step five: a decision.

50
00:02:36,274 --> 00:02:40,294
Restock it, discount it, send it
somewhere else, or write it off.

51
00:02:40,674 --> 00:02:44,714
This backward journey has
a name: reverse logistics.

52
00:02:44,714 --> 00:02:46,474
And it can be slow.

53
00:02:46,474 --> 00:02:50,434
About half of the retailers in
the NRF survey said it takes

54
00:02:50,434 --> 00:02:53,594
six to ten days to get a
returned item back in stock.

55
00:02:53,974 --> 00:02:56,604
All that time, the jacket isn't for sale.

56
00:02:56,604 --> 00:03:00,630
And if it's seasonal, it can
lose value every week it sits.

57
00:03:01,530 --> 00:03:03,000
So where does the money go?

58
00:03:03,000 --> 00:03:05,960
It helps to split the
costs into two piles.

59
00:03:06,340 --> 00:03:11,160
The first pile is operating costs: money
spent moving and handling the item.

60
00:03:11,540 --> 00:03:13,040
Return shipping.

61
00:03:13,040 --> 00:03:15,930
Labor to receive, open, and inspect.

62
00:03:15,930 --> 00:03:17,850
Cleaning, repacking, or re-tagging.

63
00:03:17,850 --> 00:03:19,380
Customer service time.

64
00:03:19,380 --> 00:03:21,470
Software and fraud checks.

65
00:03:21,850 --> 00:03:26,700
The second pile is lost value: what
the item itself is no longer worth.

66
00:03:27,080 --> 00:03:30,900
A jacket that comes back in perfect
shape, while it's still in season,

67
00:03:30,900 --> 00:03:33,520
might be resold at full price.

68
00:03:33,520 --> 00:03:39,300
One that comes back late, missing tags, or
slightly worn may have to be marked down,

69
00:03:39,300 --> 00:03:42,893
or sold off in bulk for
a fraction of its price.

70
00:03:43,273 --> 00:03:45,283
Here's a simple way to picture it.

71
00:03:45,283 --> 00:03:49,073
This is an illustrative
example, not industry data.

72
00:03:49,453 --> 00:03:52,493
Imagine a jacket that sold for $100.

73
00:03:52,493 --> 00:03:58,843
Say the return shipping costs the
seller $8, handling costs another $6,

74
00:03:58,843 --> 00:04:03,673
and because it's now out of
season, it resells for $70.

75
00:04:04,053 --> 00:04:06,053
The customer got a full refund.

76
00:04:06,053 --> 00:04:11,763
The seller spent $14 to get back
an item now worth $30 less.

77
00:04:11,763 --> 00:04:17,073
In this example, the seller is
down $44 on a single return.

78
00:04:17,453 --> 00:04:20,113
Change any assumption
and the answer changes.

79
00:04:20,113 --> 00:04:25,043
Real costs depend on the product,
the carrier contract, the warehouse,

80
00:04:25,043 --> 00:04:27,013
and the condition of the item.

81
00:04:27,393 --> 00:04:28,673
There's also fraud.

82
00:04:28,673 --> 00:04:30,493
In the NRF survey,

83
00:04:30,493 --> 00:04:36,133
retailers that track it estimated that
about 9 percent of returns were fraudulent,

84
00:04:36,133 --> 00:04:38,913
from empty boxes to swapped items.

85
00:04:38,913 --> 00:04:43,133
That's the retailers' own
estimate, not an audited number.

86
00:04:44,033 --> 00:04:45,083
So, who pays?

87
00:04:45,083 --> 00:04:48,593
The honest answer is: it
depends on who's in the chain.

88
00:04:48,973 --> 00:04:52,333
Case one: a retailer
selling its own products.

89
00:04:52,333 --> 00:04:57,173
Here, the retailer typically absorbs
the cost of a free return directly,

90
00:04:57,173 --> 00:04:58,563
at least at first.

91
00:04:58,943 --> 00:05:02,363
Case two: a marketplace
with independent sellers.

92
00:05:02,363 --> 00:05:05,663
The platform's rules decide
how costs are split,

93
00:05:05,663 --> 00:05:10,093
and some marketplaces charge
sellers fees tied to returns.

94
00:05:10,473 --> 00:05:11,943
Amazon, for example,

95
00:05:11,943 --> 00:05:18,073
has charged returns processing fees on some
products sold through its fulfillment service,

96
00:05:18,073 --> 00:05:22,673
under rules that vary by category
and have changed over time.

97
00:05:23,053 --> 00:05:25,473
Case three: logistics partners.

98
00:05:25,473 --> 00:05:29,213
Carriers and returns companies
get paid for their work.

99
00:05:29,213 --> 00:05:32,453
They're not absorbing the
cost; they're billing for it,

100
00:05:32,453 --> 00:05:35,123
under contracts the public rarely sees.

101
00:05:35,503 --> 00:05:37,483
Case four: the shopper.

102
00:05:37,483 --> 00:05:41,923
Not always through a fee on that
return, but potentially through prices,

103
00:05:41,923 --> 00:05:47,493
shipping charges, or memberships that
help cover returns across all customers.

104
00:05:47,493 --> 00:05:52,123
How much that happens varies, and it's
hard to measure from the outside.

105
00:05:52,503 --> 00:05:55,793
And more retailers are
charging shoppers directly.

106
00:05:55,793 --> 00:05:57,633
In the NRF survey,

107
00:05:57,633 --> 00:06:04,833
72 percent of merchants said they had started charging
for at least one return option in the past 12 months,

108
00:06:04,833 --> 00:06:07,673
up from 66 percent the year before.

109
00:06:08,053 --> 00:06:11,983
That can mean a fee on one return
method while another stays free,

110
00:06:11,983 --> 00:06:16,253
which nudges shoppers toward the option
that's cheaper for the retailer.

111
00:06:16,253 --> 00:06:18,203
The cost doesn't vanish.

112
00:06:18,203 --> 00:06:19,433
It gets routed.

113
00:06:20,333 --> 00:06:21,383
Back to the jacket.

114
00:06:21,383 --> 00:06:24,593
Once it's been inspected,
where does it actually go?

115
00:06:24,973 --> 00:06:29,773
If it's in sellable condition, it can
go back into stock and be sold as new.

116
00:06:30,153 --> 00:06:35,763
If it's been opened or isn't quite perfect,
it may be resold as used or open-box,

117
00:06:35,763 --> 00:06:37,673
usually at a discount.

118
00:06:38,053 --> 00:06:40,083
Some items are repaired first.

119
00:06:40,083 --> 00:06:42,573
Others are sold in bulk to liquidators,

120
00:06:42,573 --> 00:06:46,553
who move them through discount
stores and secondary markets.

121
00:06:46,933 --> 00:06:54,393
A 2025 study in the Journal of Business
Logistics noted that more than $800 billion of

122
00:06:54,393 --> 00:07:00,123
returned and excess inventory passes
through secondary market channels each year.

123
00:07:00,503 --> 00:07:06,043
Items can also go back to the original
seller, get donated, or be recycled.

124
00:07:06,423 --> 00:07:12,973
Amazon, for one, says the vast majority
of its returns are resold as new or used,

125
00:07:12,973 --> 00:07:17,373
and that items it can't resell
may be donated or recycled.

126
00:07:17,373 --> 00:07:22,093
That's the company's own account, and
it doesn't describe every retailer.

127
00:07:22,473 --> 00:07:24,453
And some returns are destroyed.

128
00:07:24,453 --> 00:07:27,733
There's no single figure
for the whole industry.

129
00:07:27,733 --> 00:07:33,963
A 2024 briefing by the European Environment
Agency estimated that in Europe,

130
00:07:33,963 --> 00:07:39,693
between 22 and 43 percent of returned
clothing bought online may end up destroyed,

131
00:07:39,693 --> 00:07:41,843
based on limited data.

132
00:07:42,223 --> 00:07:44,243
Since July 2026,

133
00:07:44,243 --> 00:07:49,783
the European Union has banned large
companies from destroying unsold clothing,

134
00:07:49,783 --> 00:07:53,051
accessories, and footwear,
with some exceptions.

135
00:07:53,951 --> 00:07:57,561
If returns cost this much, why
offer them for free at all?

136
00:07:57,941 --> 00:08:01,941
Because a generous return policy
can sell more than it costs.

137
00:08:01,941 --> 00:08:08,171
A 2016 review in the Journal of Retailing,
which pooled results from 21 studies,

138
00:08:08,171 --> 00:08:12,121
found that more lenient return
policies increased purchases.

139
00:08:12,121 --> 00:08:15,921
They increased returns too, but by less.

140
00:08:16,301 --> 00:08:20,861
Free returns lower the risk of buying
something you can't touch or try on.

141
00:08:20,861 --> 00:08:25,321
That builds trust, and trust
can turn browsers into buyers.

142
00:08:25,701 --> 00:08:27,041
But there are trade-offs.

143
00:08:27,041 --> 00:08:32,781
Lenient policies can encourage bracketing,
ordering several sizes and keeping one,

144
00:08:32,781 --> 00:08:35,407
and in some cases, outright abuse.

145
00:08:35,787 --> 00:08:40,727
So retailers tune the dials: return
windows, fees for some methods,

146
00:08:40,727 --> 00:08:45,707
exchanges instead of refunds, better
size guides, and fraud screening.

147
00:08:46,087 --> 00:08:47,627
In the NRF survey,

148
00:08:47,627 --> 00:08:53,107
retailers that added return fees reported
fewer returns and more exchanges.

149
00:08:53,107 --> 00:08:58,951
But many also reported more customer
complaints, and some said they lost customers.

150
00:08:59,331 --> 00:09:00,971
There's no free lunch here.

151
00:09:00,971 --> 00:09:04,431
Just a business decision about
which costs are worth paying.

152
00:09:05,331 --> 00:09:07,678
So, who really pays for free returns?

153
00:09:08,058 --> 00:09:10,528
Sometimes the retailer, out of its margin.

154
00:09:10,528 --> 00:09:13,808
Sometimes the seller,
through marketplace fees.

155
00:09:13,808 --> 00:09:18,078
Sometimes the shopper, through
fees on certain return methods,

156
00:09:18,078 --> 00:09:20,598
or possibly through prices.

157
00:09:20,598 --> 00:09:24,648
And part of the cost shows
up as lost value, or waste.

158
00:09:25,028 --> 00:09:28,518
What's almost never true is that
the cost simply disappears.

159
00:09:28,518 --> 00:09:33,128
Someone decides how to absorb
it, share it, or pass it along.

160
00:09:33,508 --> 00:09:38,388
So the next time you see free returns,
read it as: free for you, at that moment.

161
00:09:38,768 --> 00:09:43,738
What would you trade for an easier
return: a higher price, a shorter window,

162
00:09:43,738 --> 00:09:45,058
or a trip to the store?

163
00:09:45,438 --> 00:09:46,928
This is Hidden Ledger.

164
00:09:46,928 --> 00:09:49,778
The sources for every number
are in the description.

